package scryer:notification@1.0.0;

/// The notification-channel contract.
///
/// Both payloads are UTF-8 JSON, matching the indexer, archive, subtitle and
/// download-client worlds: `describe` returns a `PluginDescriptor` document, and
/// `process` exchanges a `scryer_plugin_sdk::command::PluginCommandRequest` for a
/// `PluginCommandResponse` — the *same* envelope the wasip1 command ABI already
/// carries on stdin/stdout, carrying `PluginCommand::Notification` and its
/// `send` and `action` operations. A guest migrating from the command ABI keeps
/// its request and response types and its dispatch `match`; only the transport
/// changes. Keeping the payload an opaque byte string is what lets the SDK add
/// notification event types and payload fields — and it has added many — without
/// a WIT revision and without re-generated bindings for the other families.
///
/// Host services arrive through the shared `scryer:host/services@1.0.0` import
/// rather than a notification-specific interface, so config, plugin state, HTTP,
/// and host-owned archive extraction reach a channel by exactly the bytes the
/// core-module ABI uses. See that package for the encoding and for why
/// capability availability is expressed in-band.
///
/// # Sockets and host processes ride the same door
///
/// This is the one family that holds authority beyond HTTP, and it reaches it
/// through *this* import rather than through a second one.
///
/// An SMTP notifier needs a raw TCP stream it can drive itself — connect, write
/// `EHLO`, read the greeting, `STARTTLS`, keep going on the upgraded stream —
/// and a first-party script notifier needs to spawn one allowlisted executable
/// on the host. Both arrive as `PluginHostRequest` variants the SDK already
/// defines: `SocketOpen`, `SocketRead`, `SocketWrite`, `SocketStartTls`,
/// `SocketClose` and `ProcessExec`. Nothing about that is visible in this WIT,
/// and that is the design: the SDK owns the capability set, so the family that
/// needs sockets imports the same one function every other family imports.
///
/// Deliberately NOT `wasi:sockets`. A p2 socket capability is ambient within
/// whatever network the host grants — the guest picks the address. Scryer's
/// grant is not ambient: every connection is checked against the resolved
/// `socket_permissions` on the plugin's own descriptor (host pattern, port set,
/// and TLS mode, with `${config_key}` patterns resolved from the channel's
/// configuration), the socket table is bounded, and reads are bounded per call
/// and in total. Handing a guest `wasi:sockets` would replace that per-channel
/// grant with a host-wide one. Process execution has no p2 equivalent at all
/// and is additionally gated to first-party plugins by the loader, which is why
/// a community notifier that declares `requires_host_process` still gets an
/// empty allowlist and a `permission_denied` on every `ProcessExec`.
///
/// A host that was not configured with these services — every other family's
/// host, and a notification host built for describe — answers `Unsupported`
/// in-band exactly as it does for any other unconfigured service. Denial by
/// descriptor permission is different and says so: it comes back as a typed
/// plugin error carrying the socket layer's own `permission_denied` code, not
/// as an absent capability.
///
/// # Preopens and WASI
///
/// WASI Preview 2 is imported implicitly: the host links the full p2 surface
/// (`wasmtime_wasi::p2::add_to_linker_async`), so a guest sees clocks, random,
/// and its captured stdio through `wasi:*` without those imports appearing in
/// this world. Notification channels receive NO filesystem preopens on any
/// operation — neither the legacy reactor nor the wasip1 command host has ever
/// handed this family a preopened directory, so a component sees precisely the
/// authority a migrating guest already had.
world notification {
  import scryer:host/services@1.0.0;

  enum invocation-error {
    failed,
    cancelled,
    invalid-response,
  }

  export describe: func() -> list<u8>;
  export process: func(request: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, invocation-error>;
}
